{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-95ef3d20a06787d5", "type": "tool_result", "content": "400\tNote Characterization Features\n401\tOnce the validated KVP detections were obtained, the corresponding segments were retrieved from the original DAS strain records and processed independently for feature extraction. Each detection was described by its energy-weighted time and frequency centroids (\n402\tT\n403\tT\n404\tand\n405\tF\n406\tF\n407\t); temporal and spectral spreads (\n408\tT\n409\tdur\n410\tT_{\\mathrm{dur}}\n411\tand\n412\tF\n413\tdur\n414\tF_{\\mathrm{dur}}\n415\t); minimum and maximum frequencies (\n416\tF\n417\tmin\n418\tF_{\\min}\n419\tand\n420\tF\n421\tmax\n422\tF_{\\max}\n423\t); mean and standard deviation of spectral energy (\n424\tE\n425\tmean\n426\tE_{\\mathrm{mean}}\n427\tand\n428\tE\n429\tstd\n430\tE_{\\mathrm{std}}\n431\t); spectral slope and its coefficient of determination (\n432\tS\n433\tS\n434\tand\n435\tS\n436\tr\n437\tS_{r}\n438\t); decay time (\n439\tT\n440\tdecay\n441\tT_{\\mathrm{decay}}\n442\t); and signal-to-noise ratio (\n443\tS\n444\t​\n445\tN\n446\t​\n447\tR\n448\tSNR\n449\t).\n450\tThis set of features was extracted following a procedure similar to the spectro-temporal pulse analysis described by\n451\tBest\n452\tet al.\n453\t(\n454\t2022\n455\t)\n456\t. For this analysis, the strain records were corrected for common-mode optical noise and filtered using a zero-phase sixth-order Butterworth band-pass filter with cutoff frequencies of 10–30 Hz. The data were then cropped using a 4-s temporal window starting 0.5 s before the KVP pick, resulting in the signal\n457\ts\n458\ts\n459\t.\n460\tThe signal\n461\ts\n462\ts\n463\twas first analyzed using two time-frequency representations: a time-oriented spectrogram providing enhanced temporal resolution and a frequency-oriented spectrogram providing enhanced frequency resolution. Because temporal and frequency resolution cannot be simultaneously maximized due to the inherent time–frequency trade-off\n464\t(Diego-Tortosa\n465\tet al.\n466\t,\n467\t2025\n468\t)\n469\t, both spectrograms were used in the subsequent analysis.\n470\tInitially, the dominant power spectral density (PSD) peak within the 16–28 Hz frequency range, corresponding to the expected fin whale note band, was identified in the temporal-oriented spectrogram . The signal\n471\ts\n472\ts\n473\twas then cropped using a 1.2 s time window centered on the time of this peak. The extracted segment, assumed to contain the fin whale note, is denoted\n474\ts\n475\tn\n476\t​\n477\to\n478\t​\n479\tt\n480\t​\n481\te\n482\ts_{note}\n483\t.\n484\tIn the temporal domain, the extracted signal\n485\ts\n486\tn\n487\t​\n488\to\n489\t​\n490\tt\n491\t​\n492\te\n493\ts_{note}\n494\twas used to compute the decay time,\n495\tT\n496\td\n497\t​\n498\te\n499\t​\n500\tc\n501\t​\n502\ta\n503\t​\n504\ty\n505\tT_{decay}\n506\t, and the\n507\tS\n508\t​\n509\tN\n510\t​\n511\tR\n512\tSNR\n513\taccording to Eq.\n514\t3\n515\t. The\n516\tS\n517\t​\n518\tN\n519\t​\n520\tR\n521\tSNR\n522\twas defined as the ratio, expressed in decibels, between the maximum absolute deviation of\n523\ts\n524\tn\n525\t​\n526\to\n527\t​\n528\tt\n529\t​\n530\te\n531\ts_{note}\n532\tfrom its median and the median absolute deviation (MAD) of\n533\ts\n534\ts\n535\t:\n536\tSNR\n537\t\\displaystyle\\mathrm{SNR}\n538\t=\n539\t20\n540\t​\n541\tlog\n542\t10\n543\t⁡\n544\t(\n545\tmax\n546\t⁡\n547\t|\n548\t𝐬\n549\tnote\n550\t−\n551\tmedian\n552\t⁡\n553\t(\n554\t𝐬\n555\tnote\n556\t)\n557\t|\n558\tMAD\n559\t⁡\n560\t(\n561\t𝐬\n562\t)\n563\t)\n564\t\\displaystyle=20\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{\\max\\left|\\mathbf{s}_{\\text{note}}-\\operatorname{median}(\\mathbf{s}_{\\text{note}})\\right|}{\\operatorname{MAD}(\\mathbf{s})}\\right)\n565\t(3)\n566\tThe decay time was estimated from the normalized absolute envelope of\n567\ts\n568\tn\n569\t​\n570\to\n571\t​\n572\tt\n573\t​\n574\te\n575\ts_{note}\n576\t, smoothed using a Savitzky–Golay filter, as the interval between the envelope maximum and the first subsequent sample at which it fell below 40% of its peak value.\n577\tThe two spectrograms were then temporally cropped to match the duration of\n578\ts\n579\tn\n580\t​\n581\to\n582\t​\n583\tt\n584\t​\n585\te\n586\ts_{note}\n587\tand restricted to the 16–28 Hz frequency range. The remaining features were extracted from these representations.\n588\tFirst, the energy-weighted time and frequency centroids of the PSD were computed using Eqs.\n589\t4\n590\tand\n591\t5\n592\t, yielding\n593\tT\n594\tT\n595\tand\n596\tF\n597\tF\n598\t, respectively. The corresponding temporal and spectral spreads,\n599\tT\n600\td\n601\t​\n602\tu\n603\t​\n604\tr\n605\tT_{dur}\n606\tand\n607\tF\n608\td\n609\t​\n610\tu\n611\t​\n612\tr\n613\tF_{dur}\n614\t, were defined as energy-weighted standard deviations scaled by a dimensionless factor\n615\tσ\n616\t\\sigma\n617\t, set to 1.5 in both the time and frequency dimensions to define the support of the signal, as described in Eq.\n618\t6\n619\t. An adaptive time–frequency mask centered at\n620\tT\n621\tT\n622\tand\n623\tF\n624\tF\n625\t, with its temporal and spectral extents determined by\n626\tT\n627\td\n628\t​\n629\tu\n630\t​\n631\tr\n632\tT_{dur}\n633\tand\n634\tF\n635\td\n636\t​\n637\tu\n638\t​\n639\tr\n640\tF_{dur}\n641\t, was used to estimate the mean spectral energy (\n642\tE\n643\tm\n644\t​\n645\te\n646\t​\n647\ta\n648\t​\n649\tn\n650\tE_{mean}\n651\t) and its standard deviation (\n652\tE\n653\ts\n654\t​\n655\tt\n656\t​\n657\td\n658\tE_{std}\n659\t), both expressed in dB. The minimum and maximum frequencies,\n660\tF\n661\tm\n662\t​\n663\ti\n664\t​\n665\tn\n666\tF_{min}\n667\tand\n668\tF\n669\tm\n670\t​\n671\ta\n672\t​\n673\tx\n674\tF_{max}\n675\t, were obtained from the extrema of the spectral ridge within the temporal support of\n676\ts\n677\tn\n678\t​\n679\to\n680\t​\n681\tt\n682\t​\n683\te\n684\ts_{note}\n685\t.\n686\tFinally, a linear regression model was fitted to this ridge, yielding the spectral slope\n687\tS\n688\tS\n689\tand its coefficient of determination\n690\tS\n691\tr\n692\tS_{r}\n693\t. The spectral ridge was defined by the frequency of maximum PSD in each time bin.\n694\tW\n695\t\\displaystyle W\n696\t=\n697\t∑\n698\ti\n699\t,\n700\tj\n701\tP\n702\ti\n703\t​\n704\tj\n705\t,\n706\t\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i,j}P_{ij},\n707\t(4)\n708\tX\n709\tc\n710\t\\displaystyle X_{c}\n711\t=\n712\t1\n713\tW\n714\t​\n715\t∑\n716\ti\n717\t,\n718\tj\n719\tP\n720\ti\n721\t​\n722\tj\n723\t​\n724\tx\n725\ti\n726\t​\n727\tj\n728\t,\n729\t\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{W}\\sum_{i,j}P_{ij}\\,x_{ij},\n730\t(5)\n731\tX\n732\tdur\n733\t\\displaystyle X_{\\mathrm{dur}}\n734\t=\n735\tσ\n736\t​\n737\t1\n738\tW\n739\t​\n740\t∑\n741\ti\n742\t,\n743\tj\n744\tP\n745\ti\n746\t​\n747\tj\n748\t​\n749\t(\n750\tx\n751\ti\n752\t​\n753\tj\n754\t−\n755\tX\n756\tc\n757\t)\n758\t2\n759\t\\displaystyle=\\sigma\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{W}\\sum_{i,j}P_{ij}\\,(x_{ij}-X_{c})^{2}}\n760\t(6)\n761\tHere\n762\tP\n763\ti\n764\t​\n765\tj\n766\tP_{ij}\n767\tdenotes the linear-scale PSD at time-frequency bin (\n768\ti\n769\ti\n770\t,\n771\tj\n772\tj\n773\t),\n774\tW\n775\tW\n776\tis the total PSD weight, and\n777\tx\n778\ti\n779\t​\n780\tj\n781\tx_{ij}\n782\tdenotes either\n783\tt\n784\tj\n785\tt_{j}\n786\tfor the temporal descriptors or\n787\tf\n788\ti\n789\tf_{i}\n790\tfor the spectral descriptors. The resulting quantities are\n791\tT\n792\tT\n793\tand\n794\tT\n795\td\n796\t​\n797\tu\n798\t​\n799\tr\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "0162f31e-a037-4d52-b8f1-9c222a9f1981", "uuid": "74e1a754-6b4e-42c2-a058-4cbd7e6d80f0", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T08:24:35.080Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/arxiv_2608.02387_text.txt", "content": "Note Characterization Features\nOnce the validated KVP detections were obtained, the corresponding segments were retrieved from the original DAS strain records and processed independently for feature extraction. Each detection was described by its energy-weighted time and frequency centroids (\nT\nT\nand\nF\nF\n); temporal and spectral spreads (\nT\ndur\nT_{\\mathrm{dur}}\nand\nF\ndur\nF_{\\mathrm{dur}}\n); minimum and maximum frequencies (\nF\nmin\nF_{\\min}\nand\nF\nmax\nF_{\\max}\n); mean and standard deviation of spectral energy (\nE\nmean\nE_{\\mathrm{mean}}\nand\nE\nstd\nE_{\\mathrm{std}}\n); spectral slope and its coefficient of determination (\nS\nS\nand\nS\nr\nS_{r}\n); decay time (\nT\ndecay\nT_{\\mathrm{decay}}\n); and signal-to-noise ratio (\nS\n​\nN\n​\nR\nSNR\n).\nThis set of features was extracted following a procedure similar to the spectro-temporal pulse analysis described by\nBest\net al.\n(\n2022\n)\n. For this analysis, the strain records were corrected for common-mode optical noise and filtered using a zero-phase sixth-order Butterworth band-pass filter with cutoff frequencies of 10–30 Hz. The data were then cropped using a 4-s temporal window starting 0.5 s before the KVP pick, resulting in the signal\ns\ns\n.\nThe signal\ns\ns\nwas first analyzed using two time-frequency representations: a time-oriented spectrogram providing enhanced temporal resolution and a frequency-oriented spectrogram providing enhanced frequency resolution. Because temporal and frequency resolution cannot be simultaneously maximized due to the inherent time–frequency trade-off\n(Diego-Tortosa\net al.\n,\n2025\n)\n, both spectrograms were used in the subsequent analysis.\nInitially, the dominant power spectral density (PSD) peak within the 16–28 Hz frequency range, corresponding to the expected fin whale note band, was identified in the temporal-oriented spectrogram . The signal\ns\ns\nwas then cropped using a 1.2 s time window centered on the time of this peak. The extracted segment, assumed to contain the fin whale note, is denoted\ns\nn\n​\no\n​\nt\n​\ne\ns_{note}\n.\nIn the temporal domain, the extracted signal\ns\nn\n​\no\n​\nt\n​\ne\ns_{note}\nwas used to compute the decay time,\nT\nd\n​\ne\n​\nc\n​\na\n​\ny\nT_{decay}\n, and the\nS\n​\nN\n​\nR\nSNR\naccording to Eq.\n3\n. The\nS\n​\nN\n​\nR\nSNR\nwas defined as the ratio, expressed in decibels, between the maximum absolute deviation of\ns\nn\n​\no\n​\nt\n​\ne\ns_{note}\nfrom its median and the median absolute deviation (MAD) of\ns\ns\n:\nSNR\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{SNR}\n=\n20\n​\nlog\n10\n⁡\n(\nmax\n⁡\n|\n𝐬\nnote\n−\nmedian\n⁡\n(\n𝐬\nnote\n)\n|\nMAD\n⁡\n(\n𝐬\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=20\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{\\max\\left|\\mathbf{s}_{\\text{note}}-\\operatorname{median}(\\mathbf{s}_{\\text{note}})\\right|}{\\operatorname{MAD}(\\mathbf{s})}\\right)\n(3)\nThe decay time was estimated from the normalized absolute envelope of\ns\nn\n​\no\n​\nt\n​\ne\ns_{note}\n, smoothed using a Savitzky–Golay filter, as the interval between the envelope maximum and the first subsequent sample at which it fell below 40% of its peak value.\nThe two spectrograms were then temporally cropped to match the duration of\ns\nn\n​\no\n​\nt\n​\ne\ns_{note}\nand restricted to the 16–28 Hz frequency range. The remaining features were extracted from these representations.\nFirst, the energy-weighted time and frequency centroids of the PSD were computed using Eqs.\n4\nand\n5\n, yielding\nT\nT\nand\nF\nF\n, respectively. The corresponding temporal and spectral spreads,\nT\nd\n​\nu\n​\nr\nT_{dur}\nand\nF\nd\n​\nu\n​\nr\nF_{dur}\n, were defined as energy-weighted standard deviations scaled by a dimensionless factor\nσ\n\\sigma\n, set to 1.5 in both the time and frequency dimensions to define the support of the signal, as described in Eq.\n6\n. An adaptive time–frequency mask centered at\nT\nT\nand\nF\nF\n, with its temporal and spectral extents determined by\nT\nd\n​\nu\n​\nr\nT_{dur}\nand\nF\nd\n​\nu\n​\nr\nF_{dur}\n, was used to estimate the mean spectral energy (\nE\nm\n​\ne\n​\na\n​\nn\nE_{mean}\n) and its standard deviation (\nE\ns\n​\nt\n​\nd\nE_{std}\n), both expressed in dB. The minimum and maximum frequencies,\nF\nm\n​\ni\n​\nn\nF_{min}\nand\nF\nm\n​\na\n​\nx\nF_{max}\n, were obtained from the extrema of the spectral ridge within the temporal support of\ns\nn\n​\no\n​\nt\n​\ne\ns_{note}\n.\nFinally, a linear regression model was fitted to this ridge, yielding the spectral slope\nS\nS\nand its coefficient of determination\nS\nr\nS_{r}\n. The spectral ridge was defined by the frequency of maximum PSD in each time bin.\nW\n\\displaystyle W\n=\n∑\ni\n,\nj\nP\ni\n​\nj\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i,j}P_{ij},\n(4)\nX\nc\n\\displaystyle X_{c}\n=\n1\nW\n​\n∑\ni\n,\nj\nP\ni\n​\nj\n​\nx\ni\n​\nj\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{W}\\sum_{i,j}P_{ij}\\,x_{ij},\n(5)\nX\ndur\n\\displaystyle X_{\\mathrm{dur}}\n=\nσ\n​\n1\nW\n​\n∑\ni\n,\nj\nP\ni\n​\nj\n​\n(\nx\ni\n​\nj\n−\nX\nc\n)\n2\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{W}\\sum_{i,j}P_{ij}\\,(x_{ij}-X_{c})^{2}}\n(6)\nHere\nP\ni\n​\nj\nP_{ij}\ndenotes the linear-scale PSD at time-frequency bin (\ni\ni\n,\nj\nj\n),\nW\nW\nis the total PSD weight, and\nx\ni\n​\nj\nx_{ij}\ndenotes either\nt\nj\nt_{j}\nfor the temporal descriptors or\nf\ni\nf_{i}\nfor the spectral descriptors. The resulting quantities are\nT\nT\nand\nT\nd\n​\nu\n​\nr", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 400, "totalLines": 1736}}}
