{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b86e6b5dc7f07726", "type": "tool_result", "content": "800\tT_{dur}\n801\tin the time dimension and\n802\tF\n803\tF\n804\tand\n805\tF\n806\td\n807\t​\n808\tu\n809\t​\n810\tr\n811\tF_{dur}\n812\tin the frequency dimension.\n813\tFor recordings sampled at 100 Hz, FFT lengths (\n814\tN\n815\tF\n816\t​\n817\tF\n818\t​\n819\tT\n820\tN_{FFT}\n821\t) of 32 and 64 were used for the time-oriented and frequency-oriented spectrograms, respectively, with an overlap of 85%. The resulting spectrogram chacracteristics are summarized in Tab.\n822\t2\n823\tDiego-Tortosa\n824\tet al.\n825\t(\n826\t2025\n827\t)\n828\t.\n829\tTable 2:\n830\tSpectrogram parameter configurations for recordings sampled at 100 Hz. Values are rounded to two decimal places.\n831\tParameter\n832\tTemporal-oriented\n833\tFrequency-oriented\n834\tN\n835\tF\n836\t​\n837\tF\n838\t​\n839\tT\n840\tN_{FFT}\n841\t32\n842\t64\n843\tOverlap [%]\n844\t85\n845\t85\n846\tTemporal resolution [ms]\n847\t48.00\n848\t96.00\n849\tFrequency resolution [Hz]\n850\t3.13\n851\t1.56\n852\tMinimum valid frequency [Hz]\n853\t6.25\n854\t3.13\n855\tThe implementation additionally supports recordings with other sampling rates. In such cases, equivalent\n856\tN\n857\tF\n858\t​\n859\tF\n860\t​\n861\tT\n862\tN_{FFT}\n863\tvalues are selected automatically to preserve spectrogram characteristics comparable to those of the 100-Hz reference configuration. A set of candidate power-of-two\n864\tN\n865\tF\n866\t​\n867\tF\n868\t​\n869\tT\n870\tN_{FFT}\n871\tvalues is generated, and the corresponding temporal resolution, frequency resolution, and minimum valid frequency are computed. The search is restricted to temporal resolutions below 100 ms, frequency resolutions below 5 Hz, and minimum valid frequencies below 10 Hz. The optimal\n872\tN\n873\tF\n874\t​\n875\tF\n876\t​\n877\tT\n878\tN_{FFT}\n879\tfor each spectrogram configuration is then selected by minimizing the relative difference from the corresponding characteristics of the 100-Hz reference spectrogram.\n880\t3.4\n881\tNote localization\n882\tFinally, source localization was performed for clusters containing KVP picks distributed along both branches of the fitted hyperbola. The presence of both branches provides stronger geometric constraints than a single-branch detection and helps reduce ambiguity in the estimated source position.\n883\tThe proposed localization method is a simplified adaptation of the approach described by\n884\tBaird\n885\tet al.\n886\t(\n887\t2025\n888\t)\n889\t, designed to reduce computational complexity. It relies exclusively on the relative arrival times measured along the DAS array. Because fin whales generally remain in the upper 50 m of the water column\n890\t(Watkins\n891\tet al.\n892\t,\n893\t1987\n894\t; Stimpert\n895\tet al.\n896\t,\n897\t2015\n898\t)\n899\t, only the horizontal source position was estimated. A two-dimensional search grid was therefore generated at a fixed source depth.\n900\tThe search grid was centred on the position of the earliest KVP detection in each cluster, which provides an initial approximation of the point along the cable closest to the source. The grid was defined by its horizontal extent and spatial resolution. In this study, a\n901\t6\n902\t​\n903\t𝐱\n904\t​\n905\t6\n906\t6\\ \\mathbf{x}\\ 6\n907\tkm\n908\t2\n909\tgrid with a spacing of 25 m in both horizontal directions was used.\n910\tOnly grid points located in water were retained. For each candidate source position, theoretical travel times to all DAS channels were calculated assuming a constant sound speed of 1500 m/s. These travel times were converted to relative arrival times by subtracting the minimum calculated travel time. Only channels containing an observed KVP pick were retained for comparison with the theoretical arrivals.\n911\tThe observed arrival times were similarly expressed relative to the earliest detection in the cluster. For each candidate source position, the calculated and observed relative arrival times were aligned by applying a uniform temporal shift that minimized their mismatch. The shift was evaluated at increments corresponding to one sampling interval. For each tested shift, the residuals between the calculated and observed arrival times were computed. Outliers were rejected using a MAD criterion, and the optimal shift was selected as that minimizing the sum of the absolute residuals for the retained observations. The localization misfit for each candidate grid point,\n912\tε\n913\t\\varepsilon\n914\t, is defined as the sum of the absolute residuals over the retained observations:\n915\tε\n916\t=\n917\t∑\n918\tk\n919\t=\n920\t1\n921\tN\n922\tin\n923\t|\n924\tT\n925\tk\n926\tcalc\n927\t−\n928\tT\n929\tk\n930\tobs\n931\t|\n932\t,\n933\t\\displaystyle\\varepsilon=\\sum_{k=1}^{N_{\\mathrm{in}}}\\left|T_{k}^{\\mathrm{calc}}-T_{k}^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\right|,\n934\t(7)\n935\twhere\n936\tT\n937\tk\n938\tcalc\n939\tT_{k}^{\\mathrm{calc}}\n940\tand\n941\tT\n942\tk\n943\tobs\n944\tT_{k}^{\\mathrm{obs}}\n945\tare the calculated and temporally shifted observed arrival times for the\n946\tk\n947\tk\n948\t-th inlier channel, respectively, and\n949\tN\n950\tin\n951\tN_{\\mathrm{in}}\n952\tis the number of retained observations.\n953\tA matching score\n954\tM\n955\tM\n956\t, was then assigned to each candidate source position. The score decreases monotonically with increasing misfit, such as the highest value corresponds to the location providing the closest agreement between calculated and observed arrival times:\n957\tM\n958\t=\n959\t1\n960\t1\n961\t+\n962\tε\n963\t2\n964\t.\n965\t\\displaystyle M=\\frac{1}{1+\\varepsilon^{2}}.\n966\t(8)\n967\tThe resulting score map was stored as a spatial representation of the relative agreement between each candidate source position and the observed arrival-time pattern.\n968\t4\n969\tResults\n970\tEight DAS recordings containing fin whale songs were used to develop and assess the proposed pipeline. These recordings were selected to represent a range of scenarios, including different whale positions relative to the fiber, variations in note structure and song duration, and one recording containing two simultaneously vocalizing individuals. Three recordings were obtained from the Tarifa deployment and five from the Estepona cable. Representative outputs from each stage of the workflow are presented below.\n971\t4.1\n972\tDetection and clustering performance\n973\tAs an illustrative example of the proposed whale-song detection workflow, a 2-h 15-min DAS recording from the Estepona cable was selected, in which a fin whale song was visually identifiable in the KVP detections.\n974\tFig.\n975\t3\n976\t.a shows the 175 039 raw KVP picks generated from the selected recording. After application of the frequency-based selection criteria described in Sec.\n977\t3.1\n978\t, 66 731 KVP picks were retained for further analysis, corresponding to a reduction of approximately 62%. A high density of detections remained within the first 5 km from the interrogator, where the background noise was strongest. Fig.\n979\t3\n980\t.b shows an enlarged view of four fin whale notes after the spatio-temporal clustering stage. This example illustrates the role of the cluster-merging procedure described in Sec.\n981\t3.2\n982\t, which consolidates adjacent clusters associated with the same acoustic event. Fig.\n983\t3\n984\t.c shows the corresponding hyperbolic fits and the subset of KVP picks retained as inliers for each note, illustrating the ability of the procedure to isolate detections associated with individual fin whale notes.\n985\t\\begin{overpic}[width=345.0pt]{Figures/Clustering.jpg}\n986\t\\put(19.0,-2.0){(a)}\n987\t\\put(53.8,-2.0){(b)}\n988\t\\put(84.5,-2.0){(c)}\n989\t\\end{overpic}\n990\tFigure 3:\n991\tKVP picks from a 2.25-h DAS recording acquired on the Estepona cable on 13 January 2024, beginning at 06:53:00 UTC.\n992\t(a)\n993\tRaw KVP picks, with the fin whale song visible between approximately 2000 and 6000 s and between 15 and 25 km along the cable.\n994\t(b)\n995\tEnlarged view of four whale notes recorded over an approximately 7-km cable section, showing the results of the spatio-temporal clustering procedure.\n996\t(c)\n997\tKVP picks retained as inliers after hyperbolic fitting. Black stars indicate the fitted hyperbola apices.\n998\tThe detection stage was quantitatively evaluated using six fin whale song recordings for which KVP picks associated with fin whale vocalizations were manually annotated. The automatic procedure, comprising spatio-temporal clustering and hyperbolic fitting as described in Sec.\n999\t3.1\n1000\t, was evaluated at both the pick and cluster levels. At the pick level, the procedure achieved a median precision of\n1001\t0.990\n1002\t0.990\n1003\t(IQR:\n1004\t0.984\n1005\t0.984\n1006\t–\n1007\t0.994\n1008\t0.994\n1009\t) and a median recall of\n1010\t0.744\n1011\t0.744\n1012\t(IQR:\n1013\t0.575\n1014\t0.575\n1015\t–\n1016\t0.879\n1017\t0.879\n1018\t). At the cluster level, the median precision was\n1019\t0.880\n1020\t0.880\n1021\t(IQR:\n1022\t0.818\n1023\t0.818\n1024\t–\n1025\t0.922\n1026\t0.922\n1027\t), whereas the median recall was\n1028\t0.806\n1029\t0.806\n1030\t(IQR:\n1031\t0.751\n1032\t0.751\n1033\t–\n1034\t0.885\n1035\t0.885\n1036\t). Thus, pick-level selection was more precise, whereas cluster-level detection achieved higher recall.\n1037\t4.2\n1038\tCharacterization of representative fin whale notes\n1039\tFollowing the identification of candidate fin whale notes, the features described in Sec.\n1040\t3.3\n1041\twere estimated from the temporal waveform, and the time- and frequency-oriented spectrograms.\n1042\tFigs.\n1043\t4\n1044\tand\n1045\t5\n1046\tshow representative type A and type B fin whale notes, respectively, extracted from DAS recordings acquired on the Estepona cable. Together, these figures illustrate the feature extraction procedure for both note types. The temporal, spectral, and energy-related descrptors are indicated in the waveform and spectrogram representations.\n1047\t\\begin{overpic}[width=345.0pt]{Figures/Figure_FWoteA.jpg}\n1048\t\\put(18.3,-3.0){(a)}\n1049\t\\put(50.7,-3.0){(b)}\n1050\t\\put(82.3,-3.0){(c)}\n1051\t\\end{overpic}\n1052\tFigure 4:\n1053\tCharacterization of a type A fin whale note recorded on the Estepona cable on 14 October 2023 at 01:41:25 UTC at channel 1739. Features extracted from\n1054\t(a)\n1055\tthe temporal waveform,\n1056\t(b)\n1057\tthe time-oriented spectrogram, and\n1058\t(c)\n1059\tthe frequency-oriented spectrogram are indicated in the corresponding titles and annotations.\n1060\t\\begin{overpic}[width=345.0pt]{Figures/Figure_FWoteB.jpg}\n1061\t\\put(18.3,-3.0){(a)}\n1062\t\\put(50.7,-3.0){(b)}\n1063\t\\put(82.3,-3.0){(c)}\n1064\t\\end{overpic}\n1065\tFigure 5:\n1066\tCharacterization of a type B fin whale note recorded on the Estepona cable on 10 January 2024 at 00:10:34 UTC at channel 3121. Features extracted from the\n1067\t(a)\n1068\tthe temporal waveform,\n1069\t(b)\n1070\tthe time-oriented spectrogram, and\n1071\t(c)\n1072\tthe frequency-oriented spectrogram are indicated in the corresponding titles and annotations.\n1073\t4.3\n1074\tLocalization of a fin whale song\n1075\tTo illustrate the localization stage, a sequence of consecutive note clusters was chosen to show the temporal evolution of the estimated source position over a complete fin whale song. Fig.\n1076\t6\n1077\t.a shows the selected clusters, which satisfied the following localization quality criteria: at least five KVP picks on each branch of the fitted hyperbola, consistent apex positions\n1078\td\n1079\t0\n1080\td_{0}\n1081\tbetween successive clusters, and spatially compact high-score regions in the matching-score maps. The sequence comprised 61 consecutive clusters spanning 33 min 42 s.\n1082\tFig.\n1083\t6\n1084\t.b shows contours corresponding to the 99\n1085\tth\n1086\tpercentile of the matching score map,\n1087\tM\n1088\tM\n1089\t, for each cluster, colored according to the apex time\n1090\tt\n1091\t0\n1092\tt_{0}\n1093\t. The temporal progression of these contours illustrates the evolution of the estimated source position during the vocalization sequence and allows the apparent direction of movement to be inferred. The localization maps retain the expected bilateral ambiguity with respect to the cable. Results are shown in a local Cartesian coordinate system whose origin is defined at the midpoint of the DAS cable at longitude\n1094\t−\n1095\t5.08078\n1096\t∘\n1097\t-5.08078^{\\circ}\n1098\tand latitude\n1099\t36.19937\n1100\t∘\n1101\t36.19937^{\\circ}\n1102\t.\n1103\t\\begin{overpic}[width=345.0pt]{Figures/win_0031.jpg}\n1104\t\\put(23.5,-2.0){(a)}\n1105\t\\put(73.0,-2.0){(b)}\n1106\t\\end{overpic}\n1107\tFigure 6:\n1108\tRepresentative localization results for a fin whale song recorded on the Estepona cable beginning on 13 January 2024 at 06:54:30 UTC.\n1109\t(a)\n1110\tSelected sequence of 61 consecutive note clusters spanning 33 min 42 s.\n1111\t(b)\n1112\tContours enclosing grid points with matching scores at or above the 99\n1113\tth\n1114\tpercentile of each cluster-specific score map, colored according to the apex time. Coordinates are expressed in a local Cartesian reference frame centered at the midpoint of the DAS cable.\n1115\t5\n1116\tDiscussion\n1117\tThe proposed pipeline was developed and evaluated using DAS measurements, but several of its processing stages are not inherently restricted to fiber-optic sensing. KVP operates on individual time series, and the note-characterization procedure is similarly based on single-receiver waveforms and spectrograms. The association and localization stages, however, require synchronized recordings from multiple receivers with known positions and sufficient spatial sampling to resolve coherent arrival-time patterns. With appropriate adaptation of these stages, the workflow could therefore be applied to other array-based acoustic systems, including conventional hydrophone arrays, cabled marine observatories, or ocean bottom seismometer (OBS) networks.\n1118\t5.1\n1119\tStrengths and limitations of the detection workflow\n1120\tUnlike many recent detection approaches, KVP uses conventional signal-processing methods rather than supervised machine learning and therefore does not requiere previously labelled data or model training. This is particularly advantageous when annotated datasets are scarce or unavailable. Conventional signal-processing methods may also offer lower computational requirements and greater interpretability than many data-driven models.\n1121\tA notable strength of KVP is its capacity to detect signals exhibiting the time–frequency characteristics represented by the selected wavelet scales, providing flexibility with respect to the specific signal source. When applied independently to the large number of channels in a DAS dataset, however, this broad sensitivity can also produce numerous non-target detections arising from spatially variable noise and cable response. An additional post-processing stage is therefore required to distinguish coherent note-related detections from isolated or incoherent picks. In the present workflow, this was achieved through spatio-temporal clustering and hyperbolic fitting, which reduced non-target detections while reatining coherent arrival patterns associated with fin whale notes.\n1122\tAn additional capability of the proposed detection workflow is the identification of simultaneous vocalizations from spatially separated sources. Following spatio-temporal clustering and hyperbolic fitting, the example shown in Fig.\n1123\t7\n1124\tcontains two temporally interleaved sequences of note clusters. One sequence has hyperbola apices located between 42 and 43 km along the cable, whereas the second has apices between 39 and 40 km. The example therefore shows that overlapping note sequences can be separated when their arrival patterns and apex positions differ sufficiently.\n1125\tNevertheless, although both song sequences can be distinguished at the detection stage, the workflow does not inherently assign individual note clusters to specific whales or reconstruct continuous movement trajectories. Such an analysis would require a subsequent stage combining source localization with track association.\n1126\tFigure 7:\n1127\tRepresentative sequence of 78 consecutive clusters corresponding to two temporally interleaved fin whale songs recorded on the Estepona cable on 15 January 2024, beginning at 09:03:02 UTC. The sequence comprises an approximately 22-min song segment and a second approximately 21-min segment, with hyperbola apices located in distinct sections of the cable, consistent with two simultaneously vocalizing individuals.\n1128\t5.2\n1129\tInterpretation and applications of note-level features\n1130\tThe features computed during the note-characterization stage are derived from two complementary time–frequency representations: a time-oriented spectrogram with enhanced temporal resolution and a frequency-oriented spectrogram with enhanced frequency resolution. Although some descriptors are estimated from both representations, the resulting values provide complementary information, particularly for distant or low-SNR detections for which reduced DAS channel sensitivity may compromise the clarity of the spectral pattern. Agreement between corresponding features obtained from both representations can therefore provide an internal consistency check, whereas substantial discrepancies may indicate an unreliable characterization.\n1131\tInstantaneous frequency estimation, following the approach proposed by\n1132\tHorne\n1133\tet al.\n1134\t(\n1135\t2025\n1136\t)\n1137\t, was also investigated for calculating\n1138\tF\n1139\tF\n1140\t,\n1141\tF\n1142\tm\n1143\t​\n1144\ti\n1145\t​\n1146\tn\n1147\tF_{min}\n1148\t, and\n1149\tF\n1150\tm\n1151\t​\n1152\ta\n1153\t​\n1154\tx\n1155\tF_{max}\n1156\t. For the present DAS dataset, however, it did not provide clearer or more stable estimates of these frequency-related features than the spectrogram-based approach.\n1157\tThe extraction of note parameters establishes a direct link between DAS detections and biologically significant acoustic information. The features are initially estimated at the channel level each inlier detection and should subsequently be interpreted at the cluster level, because all detections within a cluster are assumed to correspond to the same note. Channel-wise estimates may vary because of differences in\n1158\tS\n1159\t​\n1160\tN\n1161\t​\n1162\tR\n1163\tSNR\n1164\tand channel sensitivity. Accordingly, note interpretation should be based on the ensemble of channel-wise estimates within each cluster rather than on individual channel observations, with higher-SNR channels generally providing the most reliable characterization.\n1165\tFor high-quality detections with\n1166\tS\n1167\t​\n1168\tN\n1169\t​\n1170\tR\n1171\tSNR\n1172\tvalues greater than 8 dB, such as those shown in Figs.\n1173\t4\n1174\tand\n1175\t5\n1176\t, frequency- and slope-related features may support discrimination between type-A and type-B fin whale notes. This capability could facilitate the estimation of inter-note intervals (INIs) separately for AA, AB, BB, and BA note sequences, providing information on fin whale song structure and temporal patterns\n1177\t(Romagosa\n1178\tet al.\n1179\t,\n1180\t2024\n1181\t)\n1182\t. When two consecutive fin whale notes are detected on the same channel, their arrival-time difference provides a channel-specific interval estimate, with multiple shared channels providing replicate measurements. When consecutive notes are not captured on any common channel, the interval can instead be estimated from the difference between the apex times,\n1183\tt\n1184\t0\n1185\tt_{0}\n1186\t, of their fitted hyperbolas.\n1187\t5.3\n1188\tSource localization and movement inference\n1189\tThe localization strategy described in Sec.\n1190\t4.3\n1191\tprovides a framework for analyzing the movements of singing fin whales. The method on the selection of high-quality note clusters and the use of high-percentile contours of the matching-score maps to base the localization analysis on the most reliable detections. KVP pick times are used instead of feature\n1192\tT\n1193\tT\n1194\t, because the former show greater stability across channels, whereas\n1195\tT\n1196\tT\n1197\texhibits increased variability with decreasing signal quality and increasing source-to-channel distance.\n1198\tAlthough the matching-score maps generated for each note require additional post-processing to reconstruct whale trajectories, they already provide relevant information in their current form. As illustrated in Sec.\n1199\t4.3\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "0162f31e-a037-4d52-b8f1-9c222a9f1981", "uuid": "d68b5851-8cb0-4965-b9c6-334f65ea6736", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T08:24:50.901Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/arxiv_2608.02387_text.txt", "content": "T_{dur}\nin the time dimension and\nF\nF\nand\nF\nd\n​\nu\n​\nr\nF_{dur}\nin the frequency dimension.\nFor recordings sampled at 100 Hz, FFT lengths (\nN\nF\n​\nF\n​\nT\nN_{FFT}\n) of 32 and 64 were used for the time-oriented and frequency-oriented spectrograms, respectively, with an overlap of 85%. The resulting spectrogram chacracteristics are summarized in Tab.\n2\nDiego-Tortosa\net al.\n(\n2025\n)\n.\nTable 2:\nSpectrogram parameter configurations for recordings sampled at 100 Hz. Values are rounded to two decimal places.\nParameter\nTemporal-oriented\nFrequency-oriented\nN\nF\n​\nF\n​\nT\nN_{FFT}\n32\n64\nOverlap [%]\n85\n85\nTemporal resolution [ms]\n48.00\n96.00\nFrequency resolution [Hz]\n3.13\n1.56\nMinimum valid frequency [Hz]\n6.25\n3.13\nThe implementation additionally supports recordings with other sampling rates. In such cases, equivalent\nN\nF\n​\nF\n​\nT\nN_{FFT}\nvalues are selected automatically to preserve spectrogram characteristics comparable to those of the 100-Hz reference configuration. A set of candidate power-of-two\nN\nF\n​\nF\n​\nT\nN_{FFT}\nvalues is generated, and the corresponding temporal resolution, frequency resolution, and minimum valid frequency are computed. The search is restricted to temporal resolutions below 100 ms, frequency resolutions below 5 Hz, and minimum valid frequencies below 10 Hz. The optimal\nN\nF\n​\nF\n​\nT\nN_{FFT}\nfor each spectrogram configuration is then selected by minimizing the relative difference from the corresponding characteristics of the 100-Hz reference spectrogram.\n3.4\nNote localization\nFinally, source localization was performed for clusters containing KVP picks distributed along both branches of the fitted hyperbola. The presence of both branches provides stronger geometric constraints than a single-branch detection and helps reduce ambiguity in the estimated source position.\nThe proposed localization method is a simplified adaptation of the approach described by\nBaird\net al.\n(\n2025\n)\n, designed to reduce computational complexity. It relies exclusively on the relative arrival times measured along the DAS array. Because fin whales generally remain in the upper 50 m of the water column\n(Watkins\net al.\n,\n1987\n; Stimpert\net al.\n,\n2015\n)\n, only the horizontal source position was estimated. A two-dimensional search grid was therefore generated at a fixed source depth.\nThe search grid was centred on the position of the earliest KVP detection in each cluster, which provides an initial approximation of the point along the cable closest to the source. The grid was defined by its horizontal extent and spatial resolution. In this study, a\n6\n​\n𝐱\n​\n6\n6\\ \\mathbf{x}\\ 6\nkm\n2\ngrid with a spacing of 25 m in both horizontal directions was used.\nOnly grid points located in water were retained. For each candidate source position, theoretical travel times to all DAS channels were calculated assuming a constant sound speed of 1500 m/s. These travel times were converted to relative arrival times by subtracting the minimum calculated travel time. Only channels containing an observed KVP pick were retained for comparison with the theoretical arrivals.\nThe observed arrival times were similarly expressed relative to the earliest detection in the cluster. For each candidate source position, the calculated and observed relative arrival times were aligned by applying a uniform temporal shift that minimized their mismatch. The shift was evaluated at increments corresponding to one sampling interval. For each tested shift, the residuals between the calculated and observed arrival times were computed. Outliers were rejected using a MAD criterion, and the optimal shift was selected as that minimizing the sum of the absolute residuals for the retained observations. The localization misfit for each candidate grid point,\nε\n\\varepsilon\n, is defined as the sum of the absolute residuals over the retained observations:\nε\n=\n∑\nk\n=\n1\nN\nin\n|\nT\nk\ncalc\n−\nT\nk\nobs\n|\n,\n\\displaystyle\\varepsilon=\\sum_{k=1}^{N_{\\mathrm{in}}}\\left|T_{k}^{\\mathrm{calc}}-T_{k}^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\right|,\n(7)\nwhere\nT\nk\ncalc\nT_{k}^{\\mathrm{calc}}\nand\nT\nk\nobs\nT_{k}^{\\mathrm{obs}}\nare the calculated and temporally shifted observed arrival times for the\nk\nk\n-th inlier channel, respectively, and\nN\nin\nN_{\\mathrm{in}}\nis the number of retained observations.\nA matching score\nM\nM\n, was then assigned to each candidate source position. The score decreases monotonically with increasing misfit, such as the highest value corresponds to the location providing the closest agreement between calculated and observed arrival times:\nM\n=\n1\n1\n+\nε\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle M=\\frac{1}{1+\\varepsilon^{2}}.\n(8)\nThe resulting score map was stored as a spatial representation of the relative agreement between each candidate source position and the observed arrival-time pattern.\n4\nResults\nEight DAS recordings containing fin whale songs were used to develop and assess the proposed pipeline. These recordings were selected to represent a range of scenarios, including different whale positions relative to the fiber, variations in note structure and song duration, and one recording containing two simultaneously vocalizing individuals. Three recordings were obtained from the Tarifa deployment and five from the Estepona cable. Representative outputs from each stage of the workflow are presented below.\n4.1\nDetection and clustering performance\nAs an illustrative example of the proposed whale-song detection workflow, a 2-h 15-min DAS recording from the Estepona cable was selected, in which a fin whale song was visually identifiable in the KVP detections.\nFig.\n3\n.a shows the 175 039 raw KVP picks generated from the selected recording. After application of the frequency-based selection criteria described in Sec.\n3.1\n, 66 731 KVP picks were retained for further analysis, corresponding to a reduction of approximately 62%. A high density of detections remained within the first 5 km from the interrogator, where the background noise was strongest. Fig.\n3\n.b shows an enlarged view of four fin whale notes after the spatio-temporal clustering stage. This example illustrates the role of the cluster-merging procedure described in Sec.\n3.2\n, which consolidates adjacent clusters associated with the same acoustic event. Fig.\n3\n.c shows the corresponding hyperbolic fits and the subset of KVP picks retained as inliers for each note, illustrating the ability of the procedure to isolate detections associated with individual fin whale notes.\n\\begin{overpic}[width=345.0pt]{Figures/Clustering.jpg}\n\\put(19.0,-2.0){(a)}\n\\put(53.8,-2.0){(b)}\n\\put(84.5,-2.0){(c)}\n\\end{overpic}\nFigure 3:\nKVP picks from a 2.25-h DAS recording acquired on the Estepona cable on 13 January 2024, beginning at 06:53:00 UTC.\n(a)\nRaw KVP picks, with the fin whale song visible between approximately 2000 and 6000 s and between 15 and 25 km along the cable.\n(b)\nEnlarged view of four whale notes recorded over an approximately 7-km cable section, showing the results of the spatio-temporal clustering procedure.\n(c)\nKVP picks retained as inliers after hyperbolic fitting. Black stars indicate the fitted hyperbola apices.\nThe detection stage was quantitatively evaluated using six fin whale song recordings for which KVP picks associated with fin whale vocalizations were manually annotated. The automatic procedure, comprising spatio-temporal clustering and hyperbolic fitting as described in Sec.\n3.1\n, was evaluated at both the pick and cluster levels. At the pick level, the procedure achieved a median precision of\n0.990\n0.990\n(IQR:\n0.984\n0.984\n–\n0.994\n0.994\n) and a median recall of\n0.744\n0.744\n(IQR:\n0.575\n0.575\n–\n0.879\n0.879\n). At the cluster level, the median precision was\n0.880\n0.880\n(IQR:\n0.818\n0.818\n–\n0.922\n0.922\n), whereas the median recall was\n0.806\n0.806\n(IQR:\n0.751\n0.751\n–\n0.885\n0.885\n). Thus, pick-level selection was more precise, whereas cluster-level detection achieved higher recall.\n4.2\nCharacterization of representative fin whale notes\nFollowing the identification of candidate fin whale notes, the features described in Sec.\n3.3\nwere estimated from the temporal waveform, and the time- and frequency-oriented spectrograms.\nFigs.\n4\nand\n5\nshow representative type A and type B fin whale notes, respectively, extracted from DAS recordings acquired on the Estepona cable. Together, these figures illustrate the feature extraction procedure for both note types. The temporal, spectral, and energy-related descrptors are indicated in the waveform and spectrogram representations.\n\\begin{overpic}[width=345.0pt]{Figures/Figure_FWoteA.jpg}\n\\put(18.3,-3.0){(a)}\n\\put(50.7,-3.0){(b)}\n\\put(82.3,-3.0){(c)}\n\\end{overpic}\nFigure 4:\nCharacterization of a type A fin whale note recorded on the Estepona cable on 14 October 2023 at 01:41:25 UTC at channel 1739. Features extracted from\n(a)\nthe temporal waveform,\n(b)\nthe time-oriented spectrogram, and\n(c)\nthe frequency-oriented spectrogram are indicated in the corresponding titles and annotations.\n\\begin{overpic}[width=345.0pt]{Figures/Figure_FWoteB.jpg}\n\\put(18.3,-3.0){(a)}\n\\put(50.7,-3.0){(b)}\n\\put(82.3,-3.0){(c)}\n\\end{overpic}\nFigure 5:\nCharacterization of a type B fin whale note recorded on the Estepona cable on 10 January 2024 at 00:10:34 UTC at channel 3121. Features extracted from the\n(a)\nthe temporal waveform,\n(b)\nthe time-oriented spectrogram, and\n(c)\nthe frequency-oriented spectrogram are indicated in the corresponding titles and annotations.\n4.3\nLocalization of a fin whale song\nTo illustrate the localization stage, a sequence of consecutive note clusters was chosen to show the temporal evolution of the estimated source position over a complete fin whale song. Fig.\n6\n.a shows the selected clusters, which satisfied the following localization quality criteria: at least five KVP picks on each branch of the fitted hyperbola, consistent apex positions\nd\n0\nd_{0}\nbetween successive clusters, and spatially compact high-score regions in the matching-score maps. The sequence comprised 61 consecutive clusters spanning 33 min 42 s.\nFig.\n6\n.b shows contours corresponding to the 99\nth\npercentile of the matching score map,\nM\nM\n, for each cluster, colored according to the apex time\nt\n0\nt_{0}\n. The temporal progression of these contours illustrates the evolution of the estimated source position during the vocalization sequence and allows the apparent direction of movement to be inferred. The localization maps retain the expected bilateral ambiguity with respect to the cable. Results are shown in a local Cartesian coordinate system whose origin is defined at the midpoint of the DAS cable at longitude\n−\n5.08078\n∘\n-5.08078^{\\circ}\nand latitude\n36.19937\n∘\n36.19937^{\\circ}\n.\n\\begin{overpic}[width=345.0pt]{Figures/win_0031.jpg}\n\\put(23.5,-2.0){(a)}\n\\put(73.0,-2.0){(b)}\n\\end{overpic}\nFigure 6:\nRepresentative localization results for a fin whale song recorded on the Estepona cable beginning on 13 January 2024 at 06:54:30 UTC.\n(a)\nSelected sequence of 61 consecutive note clusters spanning 33 min 42 s.\n(b)\nContours enclosing grid points with matching scores at or above the 99\nth\npercentile of each cluster-specific score map, colored according to the apex time. Coordinates are expressed in a local Cartesian reference frame centered at the midpoint of the DAS cable.\n5\nDiscussion\nThe proposed pipeline was developed and evaluated using DAS measurements, but several of its processing stages are not inherently restricted to fiber-optic sensing. KVP operates on individual time series, and the note-characterization procedure is similarly based on single-receiver waveforms and spectrograms. The association and localization stages, however, require synchronized recordings from multiple receivers with known positions and sufficient spatial sampling to resolve coherent arrival-time patterns. With appropriate adaptation of these stages, the workflow could therefore be applied to other array-based acoustic systems, including conventional hydrophone arrays, cabled marine observatories, or ocean bottom seismometer (OBS) networks.\n5.1\nStrengths and limitations of the detection workflow\nUnlike many recent detection approaches, KVP uses conventional signal-processing methods rather than supervised machine learning and therefore does not requiere previously labelled data or model training. This is particularly advantageous when annotated datasets are scarce or unavailable. Conventional signal-processing methods may also offer lower computational requirements and greater interpretability than many data-driven models.\nA notable strength of KVP is its capacity to detect signals exhibiting the time–frequency characteristics represented by the selected wavelet scales, providing flexibility with respect to the specific signal source. When applied independently to the large number of channels in a DAS dataset, however, this broad sensitivity can also produce numerous non-target detections arising from spatially variable noise and cable response. An additional post-processing stage is therefore required to distinguish coherent note-related detections from isolated or incoherent picks. In the present workflow, this was achieved through spatio-temporal clustering and hyperbolic fitting, which reduced non-target detections while reatining coherent arrival patterns associated with fin whale notes.\nAn additional capability of the proposed detection workflow is the identification of simultaneous vocalizations from spatially separated sources. Following spatio-temporal clustering and hyperbolic fitting, the example shown in Fig.\n7\ncontains two temporally interleaved sequences of note clusters. One sequence has hyperbola apices located between 42 and 43 km along the cable, whereas the second has apices between 39 and 40 km. The example therefore shows that overlapping note sequences can be separated when their arrival patterns and apex positions differ sufficiently.\nNevertheless, although both song sequences can be distinguished at the detection stage, the workflow does not inherently assign individual note clusters to specific whales or reconstruct continuous movement trajectories. Such an analysis would require a subsequent stage combining source localization with track association.\nFigure 7:\nRepresentative sequence of 78 consecutive clusters corresponding to two temporally interleaved fin whale songs recorded on the Estepona cable on 15 January 2024, beginning at 09:03:02 UTC. The sequence comprises an approximately 22-min song segment and a second approximately 21-min segment, with hyperbola apices located in distinct sections of the cable, consistent with two simultaneously vocalizing individuals.\n5.2\nInterpretation and applications of note-level features\nThe features computed during the note-characterization stage are derived from two complementary time–frequency representations: a time-oriented spectrogram with enhanced temporal resolution and a frequency-oriented spectrogram with enhanced frequency resolution. Although some descriptors are estimated from both representations, the resulting values provide complementary information, particularly for distant or low-SNR detections for which reduced DAS channel sensitivity may compromise the clarity of the spectral pattern. Agreement between corresponding features obtained from both representations can therefore provide an internal consistency check, whereas substantial discrepancies may indicate an unreliable characterization.\nInstantaneous frequency estimation, following the approach proposed by\nHorne\net al.\n(\n2025\n)\n, was also investigated for calculating\nF\nF\n,\nF\nm\n​\ni\n​\nn\nF_{min}\n, and\nF\nm\n​\na\n​\nx\nF_{max}\n. For the present DAS dataset, however, it did not provide clearer or more stable estimates of these frequency-related features than the spectrogram-based approach.\nThe extraction of note parameters establishes a direct link between DAS detections and biologically significant acoustic information. The features are initially estimated at the channel level each inlier detection and should subsequently be interpreted at the cluster level, because all detections within a cluster are assumed to correspond to the same note. Channel-wise estimates may vary because of differences in\nS\n​\nN\n​\nR\nSNR\nand channel sensitivity. Accordingly, note interpretation should be based on the ensemble of channel-wise estimates within each cluster rather than on individual channel observations, with higher-SNR channels generally providing the most reliable characterization.\nFor high-quality detections with\nS\n​\nN\n​\nR\nSNR\nvalues greater than 8 dB, such as those shown in Figs.\n4\nand\n5\n, frequency- and slope-related features may support discrimination between type-A and type-B fin whale notes. This capability could facilitate the estimation of inter-note intervals (INIs) separately for AA, AB, BB, and BA note sequences, providing information on fin whale song structure and temporal patterns\n(Romagosa\net al.\n,\n2024\n)\n. When two consecutive fin whale notes are detected on the same channel, their arrival-time difference provides a channel-specific interval estimate, with multiple shared channels providing replicate measurements. When consecutive notes are not captured on any common channel, the interval can instead be estimated from the difference between the apex times,\nt\n0\nt_{0}\n, of their fitted hyperbolas.\n5.3\nSource localization and movement inference\nThe localization strategy described in Sec.\n4.3\nprovides a framework for analyzing the movements of singing fin whales. The method on the selection of high-quality note clusters and the use of high-percentile contours of the matching-score maps to base the localization analysis on the most reliable detections. KVP pick times are used instead of feature\nT\nT\n, because the former show greater stability across channels, whereas\nT\nT\nexhibits increased variability with decreasing signal quality and increasing source-to-channel distance.\nAlthough the matching-score maps generated for each note require additional post-processing to reconstruct whale trajectories, they already provide relevant information in their current form. As illustrated in Sec.\n4.3", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 800, "totalLines": 1736}}}
