{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bfb7f18a085c0f8d", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1200\t, the sequential localization of notes enables the general direction relative to the cable of movement during a song to be inferred, thereby facilitating the study of movement patterns adirectly from the workflow output. The localization results remain affected by the inherent bilateral ambiguity associated with a single, predominantly linear DAS cable. Nevertheless, the inferred movements correspond to biologically plausible swimming speeds. For the trajectory depicted in Fig.\n1201\t6\n1202\t.b, the estimated displacement was approximately 1861 m over the 33 min 42 s duration of the song, corresponding to an apparent swimming speed of 3.31 km/h. Similarly, the example in Fig.\n1203\t8\n1204\tcovered an estimated displacement of approximately 570 m in 5 min 16 s, corresponding to an apparent swimming speed of 6.48 km/h. Both estimates are below the 7 km/h swimming speeds typically reported for singing fin whales\n1205\t(Clark\n1206\tet al.\n1207\t,\n1208\t2019\n1209\t)\n1210\t.\n1211\t\\begin{overpic}[width=345.0pt]{Figures/win_0006.jpg}\n1212\t\\put(23.5,-2.0){(a)}\n1213\t\\put(73.0,-2.0){(b)}\n1214\t\\end{overpic}\n1215\tFigure 8:\n1216\tRepresentative localization results for a fin whale song recorded on the Estepona cable on 14 October 2023, beginning at 00:34:12 UTC.\n1217\t(a)\n1218\tSelected sequence of 11 consecutive note clusters spanning 5 min 16 s song.\n1219\t(b)\n1220\tContours enclosing grid points with matching scores at or above 99\n1221\tth\n1222\tpercentile os each cluster-specific score map. Coordinates are expressed in a local Cartesian reference frame centered at the midpoint of the DAS cable.\n1223\tThe latter example also underscores the influence of cable geometry on localization ambiguity. The reduced linearity of the cable segment in Fig.\n1224\t8\n1225\tresults in a more spatially constrained position estimate than that obtained along the predominantly straight section shown in Fig.\n1226\t6\n1227\t. This observation suggests that DAS deployments incorporating cable sections with greater directional diversity, such as curved or zig-zag layouts, or the joint interrogation of nearby cables\n1228\t(Rørstadbotnen\n1229\tet al.\n1230\t,\n1231\t2023\n1232\t)\n1233\t, could reduce the inherent localization ambiguity. Such acquisition geometries should therefore be considered when designing future DAS experiments aimed at localizing and tracking marine mammals.\n1234\tFinally, the matching score\n1235\tM\n1236\tM\n1237\tprovides a relative measure of the agreement between the calculated and observed arrival-time patterns at each candidate source position. Rather than returning a single position, the resulting score map shows the spatial distribution of candidate locations producing comparatively good fits. It therefore provides a useful representation of localization ambiguity.\n1238\t6\n1239\tConclusion\n1240\tThis work presents an automated workflow for the detection, characterization, and localization of fin whale vocalizations from raw DAS data. The proposed pipeline was applied to two DAS datasets acquired under different cable geometries and recording conditions. Its detection stage was quantitatively evaluated using manually annotated fin whale songs, while its operation does not require supervised training. The combination of KVP detection, spatio-temporal clustering, hyperbolic fitting, note characterization, and matching-score localization provides an integrated framework for transforming raw DAS measurements into interpretable bioacoustic information.\n1241\tBeyond automatic detection, the proposed workflow extracts note-level acoustic features that may support discrimination between type-A and type-B fin whale notes and estimates spatial matching-score maps for the detected sources. These outputs provide the information required to estimate the INIs for AA, AB, BA, and BB note sequences and to infer apparent swimming direction during individual songs. The score maps additionally represent the relative spatial agreement between candidate source positions and the observed arrival-time patterns, although they should not be interpreted as calibrated localization uncertainty. Overall, the workflow provides a scalable methodology for investigating marine mammal monitoring using DAS and could, with appropriate adaptation, be applied to other synchronized acoustic receiver arrays.\n1242\tSource code\n1243\tAn open-source code with the proposed pipeline is available on GitHub:\n1244\thttps://github.com/b-csi/DASWhaleCalls\n1245\t.\n1246\tAcknowledgments.\n1247\tThe contribution of Dr. Diego-Tortosa was supported by the\n1248\tALLIES Cofund Program\n1249\twhich received funding from the European Union’s\n1250\tHorizon-MSCA-2022-COFUND-01\n1251\tresearch and innovation program under the Marie Skłodowska-Curie grant agreement\n1252\tNo 101126626\n1253\t.\n1254\tThe contribution of Dra. Romagosa was supported by a Juan de la Cierva postdoctoral contract (\n1255\tJDC2023-050731-I\n1256\t).\n1257\tFunding for this project was provided through the \"Severo Ochoa\" grant for excellence\n1258\tCEX2024-001494-S\n1259\tfunded by AEI\n1260\t10.13039/501100011033\n1261\t; the European Union Next Generation EU/PRTR Program under project PSI (ref.\n1262\tPLEC2021-007875\n1263\t); the MCIN/AEI under project FERMAT (ref. PID\n1264\t2024-162301OB-C21\n1265\t); and the CSIC contract SAFE with Telxius Cable\n1266\tEspaña, S. L.\n1267\t(ref. 20233069). Telxius, and GTD Group facilitated fibre-optic cable access, and Aragon Photonics provided the HDAS interrogator used in all experiments. This work is a contribution of the Barcelona-CSI that is a Grup de Recerca 2021\n1268\tSGR 00429\n1269\tof Generalitat de Catalunya.\n1270\tReferences\n1271\tA. F. Baird, J. P. Morten, V. Oye, and S. Bjørnstad (2025)\n1272\tOcean space surveillance and real-time event characterization using distributed acoustic sensing on submarine networks\n1273\t.\n1274\tSeismological Research Letters\n1275\t96\n1276\t(\n1277\t2A\n1278\t),\n1279\tpp. 691–705\n1280\t.\n1281\tExternal Links:\n1282\tISSN 1938-2057\n1283\t,\n1284\tDocument\n1285\tCited by:\n1286\t§3.4\n1287\t.\n1288\tP. Best, R. Marxer, S. Paris, and H. Glotin (2022)\n1289\tTemporal evolution of the mediterranean fin whale song\n1290\t.\n1291\tScientific Reports\n1292\t12\n1293\t(\n1294\t1\n1295\t).\n1296\tExternal Links:\n1297\tISSN 2045-2322\n1298\t,\n1299\tDocument\n1300\tCited by:\n1301\t§3.3\n1302\t.\n1303\tL. Bouffaut (2026)\n1304\tDistributed acoustic sensing for marine mammal monitoring\n1305\t.\n1306\tESS Open Archive\n1307\t.\n1308\tNote:\n1309\tPreprint, version 1\n1310\tExternal Links:\n1311\tDocument\n1312\t,\n1313\tLink\n1314\tCited by:\n1315\t§1\n1316\t.\n1317\tM. Castellote, C. W. Clark, and M. O. Lammers (2011)\n1318\tFin whale (\n1319\tBalaenoptera physalus\n1320\t) population identity in the western Mediterranean Sea\n1321\t.\n1322\tMarine Mammal Science\n1323\t28\n1324\t(\n1325\t2\n1326\t),\n1327\tpp. 325–344\n1328\t.\n1329\tExternal Links:\n1330\tISSN 1748-7692\n1331\t,\n1332\tDocument\n1333\tCited by:\n1334\t§2\n1335\t.\n1336\tM. Castellote, C. W. Clark, and M. O. Lammers (2012)\n1337\tAcoustic and behavioural changes by fin whales (balaenoptera physalus) in response to shipping and airgun noise\n1338\t.\n1339\tBiological Conservation\n1340\t147\n1341\t(\n1342\t1\n1343\t),\n1344\tpp. 115–122\n1345\t.\n1346\tExternal Links:\n1347\tISSN 0006-3207\n1348\t,\n1349\tDocument\n1350\tCited by:\n1351\t§2\n1352\t.\n1353\tC. W. Clark, G. J. Gagnon, and A. S. Frankel (2019)\n1354\tFin whale singing decreases with increased swimming speed\n1355\t.\n1356\tRoyal Society Open Science\n1357\t6\n1358\t(\n1359\t6\n1360\t),\n1361\tpp. 180525\n1362\t.\n1363\tExternal Links:\n1364\tISSN 2054-5703\n1365\t,\n1366\tDocument\n1367\tCited by:\n1368\t§5.3\n1369\t.\n1370\tL. S