520-8524\n1449\t,\n1450\tDocument\n1451\tCited by:\n1452\t§1\n1453\t.\n1454\tA. H. Hartog (2017)\n1455\tAn introduction to distributed optical fibre sensors\n1456\t.\n1457\tCRC Press\n1458\t.\n1459\tExternal Links:\n1460\tISBN 9781315119014\n1461\t,\n1462\tDocument\n1463\tCited by:\n1464\t§1\n1465\t.\n1466\tM. Havlik, M. Predragovic, and C. M. Duarte (2022)\n1467\tState of play in marine soundscape assessments\n1468\t.\n1469\tFrontiers in Marine Science\n1470\t9\n1471\t.\n1472\tExternal Links:\n1473\tISSN 2296-7745\n1474\t,\n1475\tDocument\n1476\tCited by:\n1477\t§1\n1478\t.\n1479\tS. Horne, A. L. Stork, and F. Stanek (2025)\n1480\tObservations of fin whales and vessels offshore oregon using fibre optic distributed acoustic sensing\n1481\t.\n1482\tFrontiers in Marine Science\n1483\t12\n1484\t.\n1485\tExternal Links:\n1486\tISSN 2296-7745\n1487\t,\n1488\tDocument\n1489\tCited by:\n1490\t§1\n1491\t,\n1492\t§5.2\n1493\t.\n1494\tG. Lara, R. Miralles, M. Bou-Cabo, J. A. Esteban, and V. Espinosa (2020)\n1495\tNew insights into the design and application of a passive acoustic monitoring system for the assessment of the good environmental status in spanish marine waters\n1496\t.\n1497\tSensors\n1498\t20\n1499\t(\n1500\t18\n1501\t),\n1502\tpp. 5353\n1503\t.\n1504\tExternal Links:\n1505\tISSN 1424-8220\n1506\t,\n1507\tDocument\n1508\tCited by:\n1509\t§1\n1510\t.\n1511\tH. Latorre, S. Ventosa, A. Ugalde, A. Villaseñor, R. Bartolomé, and C. R. Ranero (2025)\n1512\tKVP: a multiscale kurtosis approach for seismic phase picking\n1513\t.\n1514\tGeophysical Journal International\n1515\t241\n1516\t(\n1517\t3\n1518\t),\n1519\tpp. 1923–1935\n1520\t.\n1521\tExternal Links:\n1522\tISSN 1365-246X\n1523\t,\n1524\tDocument\n1525\tCited by:\n1526\t§1\n1527\t,\n1528\t§3.1\n1529\t.\n1530\tT. A. Marques, L. Thomas, S. W. Martin, D. K. Mellinger, J. A. Ward, D. J. Moretti, D. Harris, and P. L. Tyack (2012)\n1531\tEstimating animal population density using passive acoustics\n1532\t.\n1533\tBiological Reviews\n1534\t88\n1535\t(\n1536\t2\n1537\t),\n1538\tpp. 287–309\n1539\t.\n1540\tExternal Links:\n1541\tISSN 1469-185X\n1542\t,\n1543\tDocument\n1544\tCited by:\n1545\t§1\n1546\t.\n1547\tH. Matsumoto, E. Araki, T. Kimura, G. Fujie, K. Shiraishi, T. Tonegawa, K. Obana, R. Arai, Y. Kaiho, Y. Nakamura, T. Yokobiki, S. Kodaira, N. Takahashi, R. Ellwood, V. Yartsev, and M. Karrenbach (2021)\n1548\tDetection of hydroacoustic signals on a fiber-optic submarine cable\n1549\t.\n1550\tScientific Reports\n1551\t11\n1552\t(\n1553\t1\n1554\t).\n1555\tExternal Links:\n1556\tISSN 2045-2322\n1557\t,\n1558\tDocument\n1559\tCited by:\n1560\t§1\n1561\t.\n1562\tN. D. Merchant, R. L. Putland, M. André, E. Baudin, M. Felli, H. Slabbekoorn, and R. Dekeling (2022)\n1563\tA decade of underwater noise research in support of the european marine strategy framework directive\n1564\t.\n1565\tOcean & Coastal Management\n1566\t228\n1567\t.\n1568\tExternal Links:\n1569\tISSN 0964-5691\n1570\t,\n1571\tDocument\n1572\tCited by:\n1573\t§1\n1574\t.\n1575\tM. Romagosa, S. Nieukirk, I. Cascão, T. A. Marques, R. Dziak, J.-Y. Royer, J. O’Brien, D. K. Mellinger, A. Pereira, A. Ugalde, E. Papale, S. Aniceto, G. Buscaino, M. Rasmussen, L. Matias, R. Prieto, and M. A. Silva (2024)\n1576\tFin whale song evolution in the North Atlantic\n1577\t.\n1578\teLife\n1579\t13\n1580\t.\n1581\tExternal Links:\n1582\tISSN 2050-084X\n1583\t,\n1584\tDocument\n1585\tCited by:\n1586\t§5.2\n1587\t.\n1588\tR. A. Rørstadbotnen, J. Eidsvik, L. Bouffaut, M. Landrø, J. Potter, K. Taweesintananon, S. Johansen, F. Storevik, J. Jacobsen, O. Schjelderup, S. Wienecke, T. A. Johansen, B. O. Ruud, A. Wuestefeld, and V. Oye (2023)\n1589\tSimultaneous tracking of multiple whales using two fiber-optic cables in the arctic\n1590\t.\n1591\tFrontiers in Marine Science\n1592\t10\n1593\t.\n1594\tExternal Links:\n1595\tISSN 2296-7745\n1596\t,\n1597\tDocument\n1598\tCited by:\n1599\t§5.3\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "0162f31e-a037-4d52-b8f1-9c222a9f1981", "uuid": "85d48963-45c3-4c5e-808a-23624f75b551", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T08:25:04.259Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/arxiv_2608.02387_text.txt", "content": ", the sequential localization of notes enables the general direction relative to the cable of movement during a song to be inferred, thereby facilitating the study of movement patterns adirectly from the workflow output. The localization results remain affected by the inherent bilateral ambiguity associated with a single, predominantly linear DAS cable. Nevertheless, the inferred movements correspond to biologically plausible swimming speeds. For the trajectory depicted in Fig.\n6\n.b, the estimated displacement was approximately 1861 m over the 33 min 42 s duration of the song, corresponding to an apparent swimming speed of 3.31 km/h. Similarly, the example in Fig.\n8\ncovered an estimated displacement of approximately 570 m in 5 min 16 s, corresponding to an apparent swimming speed of 6.48 km/h. Both estimates are below the 7 km/h swimming speeds typically reported for singing fin whales\n(Clark\net al.\n,\n2019\n)\n.\n\\begin{overpic}[width=345.0pt]{Figures/win_0006.jpg}\n\\put(23.5,-2.0){(a)}\n\\put(73.0,-2.0){(b)}\n\\end{overpic}\nFigure 8:\nRepresentative localization results for a fin whale song recorded on the Estepona cable on 14 October 2023, beginning at 00:34:12 UTC.\n(a)\nSelected sequence of 11 consecutive note clusters spanning 5 min 16 s song.\n(b)\nContours enclosing grid points with matching scores at or above 99\nth\npercentile os each cluster-specific score map. Coordinates are expressed in a local Cartesian reference frame centered at the midpoint of the DAS cable.\nThe latter example also underscores the influence of cable geometry on localization ambiguity. The reduced linearity of the cable segment in Fig.\n8\nresults in a more spatially constrained position estimate than that obtained along the predominantly straight section shown in Fig.\n6\n. This observation suggests that DAS deployments incorporating cable sections with greater directional diversity, such as curved or zig-zag layouts, or the joint interrogation of nearby cables\n(Rørstadbotnen\net al.\n,\n2023\n)\n, could reduce the inherent localization ambiguity. Such acquisition geometries should therefore be considered when designing future DAS experiments aimed at localizing and tracking marine mammals.\nFinally, the matching score\nM\nM\nprovides a relative measure of the agreement between the calculated and observed arrival-time patterns at each candidate source position. Rather than returning a single position, the resulting score map shows the spatial distribution of candidate locations producing comparatively good fits. It therefore provides a useful representation of localization ambiguity.\n6\nConclusion\nThis work presents an automated workflow for the detection, characterization, and localization of fin whale vocalizations from raw DAS data. The proposed pipeline was applied to two DAS datasets acquired under different cable geometries and recording conditions. Its detection stage was quantitatively evaluated using manually annotated fin whale songs, while its operation does not require supervised training. The combination of KVP detection, spatio-temporal clustering, hyperbolic fitting, note characterization, and matching-score localization provides an integrated framework for transforming raw DAS measurements into interpretable bioacoustic information.\nBeyond automatic detection, the proposed workflow extracts note-level acoustic features that may support discrimination between type-A and type-B fin whale notes and estimates spatial matching-score maps for the detected sources. These outputs provide the information required to estimate the INIs for AA, AB, BA, and BB note sequences and to infer apparent swimming direction during individual songs. The score maps additionally represent the relative spatial agreement between candidate source positions and the observed arrival-time patterns, although they should not be interpreted as calibrated localization uncertainty. Overall, the workflow provides a scalable methodology for investigating marine mammal monitoring using DAS and could, with appropriate adaptation, be applied to other synchronized acoustic receiver arrays.\nSource code\nAn open-source code with the proposed pipeline is available on GitHub:\nhttps://github.com/b-csi/DASWhaleCalls\n.\nAcknowledgments.\nThe contribution of Dr. Diego-Tortosa was supported by the\nALLIES Cofund Program\nwhich received funding from the European Union’s\nHorizon-MSCA-2022-COFUND-01\nresearch and innovation program under the Marie Skłodowska-Curie grant agreement\nNo 101126626\n.\nThe contribution of Dra. Romagosa was supported by a Juan de la Cierva postdoctoral contract (\nJDC2023-050731-I\n).\nFunding for this project was provided through the \"Severo Ochoa\" grant for excellence\nCEX2024-001494-S\nfunded by AEI\n10.13039/501100011033\n; the European Union Next Generation EU/PRTR Program under project PSI (ref.\nPLEC2021-007875\n); the MCIN/AEI under project FERMAT (ref. PID\n2024-162301OB-C21\n); and the CSIC contract SAFE with Telxius Cable\nEspaña, S. L.\n(ref. 20233069). Telxius, and GTD Group facilitated fibre-optic cable access, and Aragon Photonics provided the HDAS interrogator used in all experiments. This work is a contribution of the Barcelona-CSI that is a Grup de Recerca 2021\nSGR 00429\nof Generalitat de Catalunya.\nReferences\nA. F. Baird, J. P. Morten, V. Oye, and S. Bjørnstad (2025)\nOcean space surveillance and real-time event characterization using distributed acoustic sensing on submarine networks\n.\nSeismological Research Letters\n96\n(\n2A\n),\npp. 691–705\n.\nExternal Links:\nISSN 1938-2057\n,\nDocument\nCited by:\n§3.4\n.\nP. Best, R. Marxer, S. Paris, and H. Glotin (2022)\nTemporal evolution of the mediterranean fin whale song\n.\nScientific Reports\n12\n(\n1\n).\nExternal Links:\nISSN 2045-2322\n,\nDocument\nCited by:\n§3.3\n.\nL. Bouffaut (2026)\nDistributed acoustic sensing for marine mammal monitoring\n.\nESS Open Archive\n.\nNote:\nPreprint, version 1\nExternal Links:\nDocument\n,\nLink\nCited by:\n§1\n.\nM. Castellote, C. W. Clark, and M. O. Lammers (2011)\nFin whale (\nBalaenoptera physalus\n) population identity in the western Mediterranean Sea\n.\nMarine Mammal Science\n28\n(\n2\n),\npp. 325–344\n.\nExternal Links:\nISSN 1748-7692\n,\nDocument\nCited by:\n§2\n.\nM. Castellote, C. W. Clark, and M. O. Lammers (2012)\nAcoustic and behavioural changes by fin whales (balaenoptera physalus) in response to shipping and airgun noise\n.\nBiological Conservation\n147\n(\n1\n),\npp. 115–122\n.\nExternal Links:\nISSN 0006-3207\n,\nDocument\nCited by:\n§2\n.\nC. W. Clark, G. J. Gagnon, and A. S. Frankel (2019)\nFin whale singing decreases with increased swimming speed\n.\nRoyal Society Open Science\n6\n(\n6\n),\npp. 180525\n.\nExternal Links:\nISSN 2054-5703\n,\nDocument\nCited by:\n§5.3\n.\nL. S. Di Mauro, D. Diego-Tortosa, V. Sciacca, G. Riccobene, and S. Viola (2025)\nAcoustic Tracking of Sperm Whales (Physeter macrocephalus) in the Central Mediterranean Sea Using the NEMO-O\nν\n\\nu\nDE Deep-Sea Observatory\n.\nJournal of Marine Science and Engineering\n13\n(\n4\n),\npp. 682\n.\nExternal Links:\nISSN 2077-1312\n,\nDocument\nCited by:\n§1\n.\nD. Diego-Tortosa, D. Bonanno, M. Bou-Cabo, L. S. Di Mauro, A. Idrissi, G. Lara, G. Riccobene, S. Sanfilippo, and S. Viola (2025)\nEffective strategies for automatic analysis of acoustic signals in long-term monitoring\n.\nJournal of Marine Scien